mics to address  social and political issues at the time  including the vietnam war racism and  drug abuse when lee was asked by the us  department of health to write a book  about the dangers of drug use  lee wrote a story that saw one of  spider-man's friends become addicted to  drugs but due to its depiction of drug  use the comics code authority refused to  approve it for publication now at that  time the comey's code authority seal  appeared on the cover of nearly every  comic book that was published to verify  that the contents inside were not too  graphic for young readers and so instead  of changing the issue to comply with the  comics code lee published the book  without the seal which led to the code  making changes to what  they would allow to be published under  their seal now in addition to infusing  political and social issues into the  stories themselves  lee penned an editorial column known as  stan soapbox that he would use  to bring to light issues such as racial  injustice and discrimination now lee  stopped writing comics regularly for  marvel in 1972 but remained with the  company as by that time he'd become  somewhat of a celebrity and the  unofficial face of the company the  official face was spider-man but lee  stepped down from his role as  editor-in-chief as well opting to take  more of a symbolic  role as a publisher now roy thomas  succeededly as editor-in-chief and lee  spent much of that time  making public appearances at comic book  conventions as well as making media  appearances  and was regularly invited to speak at  college campuses as marvel comics had  become increasingly popular  with college students all the college  kids would just take acid and read  doctor strange comics that's why he was  so popular but seeking to bolster  marvel's television presence stan moved  to california in 1981 but his effort had  varying degrees of success the  incredible hulk starring lou ferrigno  and bill bixby was a huge hit and  several marvel 